<div class="relative isolate overflow-hidden">
  <svg class="absolute inset-0 -z-10 h-full w-full stroke-yellow-200 [mask-image:radial-gradient(100%_100%_at_top_right,white,transparent)]" aria-hidden="true">
    <defs>
      <pattern id="0787a7c5-978c-4f66-83c7-11c213f99cb7" width="52" height="52" x="50%" y="-1" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
        <path id="Combined-Shape" d="M0 38.59l2.83-2.83 1.41 1.41L1.41 40H0v-1.41zM0 1.4l2.83 2.83 1.41-1.41L1.41 0H0v1.41zM38.59 40l-2.83-2.83 1.41-1.41L40 38.59V40h-1.41zM40 1.41l-2.83 2.83-1.41-1.41L38.59 0H40v1.41zM20 18.6l2.83-2.83 1.41 1.41L21.41 20l2.83 2.83-1.41 1.41L20 21.41l-2.83 2.83-1.41-1.41L18.59 20l-2.83-2.83 1.41-1.41L20 18.59z" />
      </pattern>
    </defs>
    <rect width="100%" height="100%" stroke-width="0" fill="url(#0787a7c5-978c-4f66-83c7-11c213f99cb7)" />
  </svg>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-6 pb-24 pt-10 sm:pb-32 lg:flex lg:px-8 lg:py-40">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl lg:mx-0 lg:max-w-xl lg:flex-shrink-0 lg:pt-8">
      <%# The connector leads, which is #255 answered with option 1: this page used to
          open "Lift heavier" and describe a workout logger, which is what Hevy, Strong
          and Boostcamp all say, most of them with more polish and all of them with more
          reviews. On that ground this app is one of many. The connector is the one thing
          none of them has, and it was the third sentence, in the same size and weight as
          the two above it, written as a feature ("connect X to do Y") rather than as
          something a person would want, and using two words -- "MCP" and "your LLM" --
          that ask the reader to already be in this industry.

          Saying "your AI" rather than naming Claude, which is #326 reversing what #255
          decided here. The argument for the name was that people who talk to one of these
          think of it by name -- true, and it costs more than it buys. A headline naming one
          vendor reads as a product built for that vendor, which this is not: it speaks MCP,
          and what supports MCP is a list that has grown since this page was written and
          will keep growing. "Your AI" is right for every reader on that list, including the
          ones whose assistant did not exist when the sentence was written.

          The names are kept in exactly one place -- the per-client setup steps on
          /connections -- because there the name *is* the instruction: "Settings, then
          Connectors" is a different sequence in each one, and a generic word would leave a
          reader hunting.

          The audience this narrows to is smaller than "people who lift" and that is
          deliberate rather than accidental. Hedging cost both: the broad claim loses on
          its own terms against apps with a decade of reviews, and the narrow one was
          buried under it.

          Five words rather than two, so the size steps down a notch at every breakpoint
          from what "Lift heavier" could carry. %>
      <h1 class="serif mt-10 text-5xl font-bold text-sky-800 sm:text-6xl md:text-7xl">Ask your AI what to lift</h1>
      <%# The serif carries on past the headline, which is #200. This is the one page whose
          job is tone rather than use -- nobody loads a bar off it -- so the face belongs to
          the whole of its voice rather than to its first line, which is where it stopped
          and looked like an accident. The buttons below are controls and stay in the
          system face; the rule is the page, not everything on it. %>
      <p class="serif mt-6 text-lg leading-8 text-gray-600">Connect tectonic plates to your AI assistant and it writes your next block, changes it when the week does, and reads back what you actually lifted.</p>
      <p class="serif mt-4 text-lg leading-8 text-gray-600">Underneath it is a lifting log: your bar, your plates, and your heaviest lifts charted day by day.</p>
      <div class="mt-10 flex items-center gap-x-6">
        <%# Log in rests a step darker than the primary button everywhere else, so its hover is
            lime-700 rather than the usual lime-600. It hovered to lime-500 before, which made it
            the one button in the app that got lighter when you pointed at it. %>
        <a href="/login" class="<%= button_style %> bg-lime-600 px-3.5 py-2.5 text-sm font-semibold text-white hover:bg-lime-700">Log in</a>
        <a href="/create-account" class="<%= button_style %> bg-sky-800 px-3.5 py-2.5 text-sm font-semibold text-white hover:bg-sky-900">Sign up</a>
        <%# This said "Learn more" and went to /about, which is a colophon: a name, a
            twitter handle and the Noun Project icon credits. The reader most likely to
            sign up is the one who clicks it, and they were sent to attribution for the
            squat icon. It now goes to the thing they were asking for, which is on this
            page. /about keeps its link at the foot, because the icon licence turns on
            that credit being reachable. %>
        <a href="#how-it-works" class="text-sm font-semibold leading-6 text-sky-800">See how it works <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <%# Shown at every width, which is #253. It was `invisible lg:visible` on the container
        and `w-[0rem] lg:w-[16rem]` on the image -- two mechanisms saying the same thing,
        neither removed, and `invisible` reserves the layout box that `hidden` does not, so
        the page also carried an empty column on a phone. Below 1024px the marketing page
        for a gym app showed no picture of the app, on the device the app is used on and
        the device a link between two lifters is most likely opened on.

        The reason to hide it was that the shot was unreadable small. It is not: the file
        is a 500px-wide portrait screenshot of the session screen, which is the most
        legible surface this app has -- a weight, a rep count, the plates that make it and
        a Done button. It reads at 320px in a way the workout table never would.

        The width and height attributes said 2432x1442 while the file was 591x1280. That
        is not cosmetic: the browser reserves a box of that shape before the image arrives,
        so the page laid out landscape and then jumped when a portrait image landed in it.
        They are the file's own numbers now, and welcome_spec reads the file to keep them
        that way. %>
    <div class="mx-auto mt-16 flex max-w-2xl justify-center sm:mt-20 lg:ml-10 lg:mr-0 lg:mt-0 lg:max-w-none lg:flex-none xl:ml-32">
      <div class="max-w-3xl flex-none sm:max-w-5xl lg:max-w-none">
        <div class="-m-2 rounded-xl bg-gray-900/5 p-2 ring-1 ring-inset ring-gray-900/10 lg:-m-4 lg:rounded-2xl lg:p-4">
          <img src="/img/screenshot.jpeg" alt="A session in progress: Back Squat at 225 for 5, the plates that make it, and a Done button." width="500" height="992" class="w-56 sm:w-64 lg:w-[16rem] xl:w-[22rem] rounded-md shadow-2xl ring-1 ring-gray-900/10">
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<%# The section the headline promises, and the one #255 asked for: "a real exchange -- ask
    for next week, get a programme". It is here rather than on /about because the reader
    who wants it is the reader deciding whether to sign up, and sending them to another
    page to find out what the product does loses most of them.

    Every step below is a thing the connector actually does today, which is the whole
    point of showing it: create_program writes a block in one call, generate_program_week
    turns a week of it into sessions on the calendar, update_program_lift changes a lift
    and rewrites the sessions already generated from it, and exercise_history is where the
    numbers come from. Nothing here is a mockup of something planned. %>
<section id="how-it-works" class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-6 pb-24 lg:px-8">
  <h2 class="serif text-3xl font-semibold text-sky-800">Ask for next week. Get next week.</h2>
  <p class="serif mt-4 text-lg leading-8 text-gray-600">A real exchange, and every step in it is something the connector does today.</p>

  <div class="mt-8 space-y-4">
    <div class="rounded-2xl bg-white p-5 shadow-sm ring-1 ring-inset ring-gray-900/10">
      <%# Small caps in gray-500 is this app's label register: the session screen wears it
          on Warmup, RPE and Plate math, so a label always reads as a label and never as a
          value. The same rule here means the speaker never reads as part of the sentence. %>
      <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500">You</p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-gray-900">I&rsquo;m coming back after six weeks off. Write me a four-week squat block, three days a week.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="rounded-2xl bg-sky-800 p-5 text-white">
      <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-sky-200">Your AI</p>
      <p class="mt-2">Four weeks from Monday. Monday opens at 150 and steps to 165 &mdash; every weight loadable on the plates you own.</p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-sky-200">read your history &middot; wrote the block &middot; generated week one</p>
    </div>

    <div class="rounded-2xl bg-white p-5 shadow-sm ring-1 ring-inset ring-gray-900/10">
      <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500">You</p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-gray-900">Make Wednesday&rsquo;s squats flat sets rather than ascending.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="rounded-2xl bg-sky-800 p-5 text-white">
      <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-sky-200">Your AI</p>
      <p class="mt-2">Done &mdash; Wednesday is four sets of five at 155, and the sessions already on your calendar have been rewritten to match.</p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-sky-200">edited the lift &middot; rewrote three sessions</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <%# "MCP" appeared on this page with nothing explaining it, which asks the reader to
      already know an acronym that is about two years old outside this industry. It is
      still named, because somebody who does know it is looking for exactly that word --
      but it is named after the thing it does rather than before it. %>
  <p class="serif mt-8 text-lg leading-8 text-gray-600">It works over MCP, the connector standard the major AI assistants speak. You authorise it once, and you can take the authorisation back whenever you like.</p>

  <%# "Create an account" rather than a second "Sign up". The words are the same offer, but
      four specs reach the sign-up form by asking Capybara for the button called Sign up on
      this page, and two of them is ambiguous -- a marketing choice should not make the
      suite less specific about what it is clicking. %>
  <div class="mt-10 flex items-center gap-x-6">
    <a href="/create-account" class="<%= button_style %> bg-sky-800 px-3.5 py-2.5 text-sm font-semibold text-white hover:bg-sky-900">Create an account</a>
    <a href="/about" class="text-sm font-semibold leading-6 text-sky-800">About this app <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></a>
  </div>
</section>
